{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of outcomes relative to the mean. A lottery is left-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher values) compared to unfavorable ones (lower values). In this case, Lottery A has a 66% chance of 2400€ and a 33% chance of 2500€, with a negligible 1% chance of 0€. This means most of the probability mass is on the higher end (2400€ and 2500€), suggesting a left-skewed distribution. Lottery B offers 2400€ with 100% probability — a single point mass, which is symmetric (no skewness) or possibly right-skewed depending on interpretation, but not left-skewed because there is no concentration of probability on favorable outcomes beyond the certain value. Thus, A is more left-skewed than B. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which is A. Therefore, the reason can be applied to this decision problem and would prefer A over B.",
  "step_2": "The individual states they chose Lottery B because they receive 2400€ for sure. They express a preference for a certain outcome (100% probability) over a risky one, even if it means receiving 100€ less than what might be possible in A. The individual does not mention probabilities, outcomes, or skewness in their reasoning. Their justification is based on risk aversion and the desire for certainty, not on the distributional shape (skewness) of the lotteries. They do not discuss the probability distribution of outcomes or compare the asymmetry of the outcomes. Thus, the deliberation process focuses on certainty and risk preference, not on skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the distribution of outcomes, the probability weights on favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes, or any assessment of left-skewness. Instead, the justification centers on certainty and risk aversion. The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness — specifically, whether a lottery has a greater probability mass on favorable outcomes — which was not part of the individual’s reasoning. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The final assessment is that the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if more probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher monetary outcomes) compared to unfavorable ones (zero). In this case, Lottery A has a 33% chance of 2500 Euros and 67% chance of 0 Euros. Lottery B has a 34% chance of 2400 Euros and 66% chance of 0 Euros. The favorable outcome (non-zero) is higher in A (2500 vs. 2400), and the probability of the favorable outcome is slightly lower in A (33% vs. 34%). Thus, A has a larger weight on the higher outcome (2500) relative to the zero outcome (67% vs. 66%). This means A has a greater concentration of probability on the favorable outcome — a more left-skewed distribution. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied: A is more left-skewed than B, so the reason would prefer A.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Ich nehme A da der Betrag 100€ mehr war. 0,1% unterschiede bei der Wahrscheinlichkeit sind für mich weiterhin nicht gross von Bedeutung.' This translates to: 'I choose A because the amount was 100€ more. The 0.1% difference in probability is not significant to me.' The individual focuses solely on the difference in the monetary outcome (2500€ vs. 2400€), noting that the 100€ difference is what matters. The probability difference (33% vs. 34%) is considered negligible. No mention is made of skewness, distribution, or the shape of the probability mass. The justification is based on the absolute value of the monetary gain, not on the structure of the probability distribution or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the skewness of the lotteries — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not refer to the distribution of probabilities or the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. Instead, the justification is based purely on the absolute difference in the monetary payoff (100€) and the insignificance of the probability difference. The decision reason's rule — preferring the more left-skewed lottery — is not reflected in the reported deliberation. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The final assessment is 0.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

